{"by":"brianrhea","descendants":110,"id":50541568,"kids":[50574304,50587183,50573909,50573254,50573439,50576890,50574360,50572944,50577161,50573730,50573162,50577803,50574624,50573127,50574637,50545892,50581642,50574648,50578406,50572898,50583895,50579625,50579460,50574354,50573590,50579689,50575958,50574001,50573954,50573866,50541605,50576453,50574186],"score":165,"text":"I&#x27;ve had tinnitus for about a year and a half. There&#x27;s no cure, so I&#x27;m trying anything that looks promising. One idea people are researching is listening to sound with a notch cut out right at your tinnitus frequency.<p>The frequency finder will help you zero in on your tinnitus pitch. The method write-up is here: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;trynotched.com&#x2F;how-pitch-matching-works\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;trynotched.com&#x2F;how-pitch-matching-works</a><p>Once you have a pitch, there&#x27;s a free web app that plays background sounds (stream, rain, wind and so on) with a notch at your frequency: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;trynotched.com&#x2F;tools\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;trynotched.com&#x2F;tools</a>.<p>I built it as a PWA so you can save to your phone&#x27;s home screen and it works offline. No account or time limit.","time":1794973201,"title":"Show HN: Tinnitus frequency finder and audio notch tool","type":"story","url":"https://trynotched.com/find"}
